          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#General General
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+quantum+field+theory"} relativistic quantum field theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            there are three essential choices of forms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypersurfaces"} hypersurfaces
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            by &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Dirac49"}> Dirac (1949)
            \&amp;rbrack;:
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/DiracFormsOfHamiltonianDynamics.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              (Fig. 1 from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#BrodskyPauliPinsky98"} Brodsky, Pauli &amp; Pinsky (1998)
          %p
            The “instant form” on the left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypersurfaces"} hypersurfaces
            (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            in most of the literature (certainly in the case of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "&rbrack;)." do
              %a{:href => "#Dirac49"} Dirac (1949) §3
          %p
            In contrast,
            %em light-cone quantization
            corresponds to the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefronts"} wavefronts
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Dirac49"}> Dirac (1949) §5
            \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Dirac49"}> Dirac (1949) §8
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p The instant form has the advantage of being the one people are most familiar with, but I do not believe it is intrinsically any better for this reason. &amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack;
            %p The front form has the advantage that it requires only three Hamiltonians, instead of the four of the other forms. This makes it mathematically the most interesting form, and makes any problem of finding Hamiltonians substantially easier.
            %p The front form has the further advantage that there is no square root in the Hamiltonians, which means that one can avoid negative energies for particles by suitably choosing the values of the dynamical variables in the front, without having to make a special convention about the sign of a square root.
          %p (…)
          %h3#DiscreteLightConeQuantization Discretized light-cone quantization
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonperturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            computations it turns out at least convenient (if not necessary) to in addition assume/impose periodic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
            along the lightlike front, hence, in flat lightcone coordinates
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo ±
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^{\pm}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            satisfy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi\big(
                  x^- + L,\, x^+
                  \\big)
                  \\;=\;
                  \\phi\big(
                  x^-,\, x^+
                  \\big)
          %p
            for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \in \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0}
            giving the circumference of a lightlike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            (which some authors take to be literally
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "Lorentizan+geometry#Bär04"} Bär 2004, Exp. 2.21 (2)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Seiberg97"} Seiberg 1997
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boosts"} boosts
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
          %p
            Since,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} dually
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^+
            becomes quantized in units of the inverse
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            of this circle
          #eq:LightConeMomentumQuantization.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∝
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi R
                  %mphantom
                    %mi AAA
                  %mi N
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p^+ \;\propto\; N/R
                  \\phantom{AAA}
                  N \in \mathbb{Z}
                  \\,,
          %p
            hence one speaks of
            %em discretized light-cone quantization
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em DLCQ
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#MaskawaYamakawi76"} Maskawa &amp; Yamakawi 1976
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Casher76"} Casher 1976
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Thorn77"} Thorn 1977
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Thorn78"} 1978
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#PauliBrodsky85b"} Pauli &amp; Brodsky (1985b)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#PauliBrodsky85a"} (1985a)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#TangBrodskyPauli91"} Tang, Brodsky &amp; Pauli 1991 (2.6)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#McCartorRobertson94"} McCartor &amp; Robertson 1994, §3
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Burkardt96"} Burkardt 1996, §5.1
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Heinzl01"} Heinzl 2001, §3.4
          %p
            Often it turns out that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+number"} negative
            values of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            in
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:LightConeMomentumQuantization"} (1)
            can be neglected or integrated out, so that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  N \in \mathbb{N}
          %p
            becomes a
            = succeed "-parameter" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+double+line+construction"} 't Hooft double line construction
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            of the discrete light cone quantization becomes of interest.
          %h3#for_sigmamodels For sigma-models
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+models"} sigma models
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector"} Killing vector
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fix
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            such that also the worldvolume has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector+field"} Killing vector field
            and such that this is mapped to the given one on target space.
          %p
            This typically fixes most of the available gauge freedom, and the strategy is then to apply
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            to what remains. For more on this general idea see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+commutes+with+reduction"} quantization commutes with reduction
          %p
            Often this is considered for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
